<item>
  <title>Why Two-Step Transition Probabilities Form P²</title>
  <dc:creator>Mingu Kang</dc:creator>
  <link>https://mkw0rld.github.io/posts/Junior-summer/Lab internship/Essentials of Stochastic Processes/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<section id="the-question" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-question">The question</h2>
<p>Let (P) be the transition matrix of a Markov chain.</p>
<p>Why does the ((i,j))-entry of (P^2) represent the probability of moving from state (i) to state (j) in two steps?</p>
</section>
<section id="one-step-transition-probabilities" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="one-step-transition-probabilities">One-step transition probabilities</h2>
<p>The one-step transition probability is defined by</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0Ap(i,j)%0A=%0AP(X_%7Bn+1%7D=j%20%5Cmid%20X_n=i).%0A"></p>
<p>This is the probability that the chain moves from state (i) to state (j) in one step.</p>
</section>
<section id="two-step-transition-probabilities" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="two-step-transition-probabilities">Two-step transition probabilities</h2>
<p>Suppose that the chain begins at state (i) and reaches state (j) after two steps.</p>
<p>At the intermediate step, the chain may visit any state (k). Therefore,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AP(X_%7Bn+2%7D=j%20%5Cmid%20X_n=i)%0A=%0A%5Csum_%7Bk%5Cin%20S%7D%0AP(X_%7Bn+2%7D=j,%20X_%7Bn+1%7D=k%20%5Cmid%20X_n=i).%0A"></p>
<p>Using the Markov property,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AP(X_%7Bn+2%7D=j%20%5Cmid%20X_%7Bn+1%7D=k,%20X_n=i)%0A=%0AP(X_%7Bn+2%7D=j%20%5Cmid%20X_%7Bn+1%7D=k).%0A"></p>
<p>Hence,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0Ap%5E%7B(2)%7D(i,j)%0A=%0A%5Csum_%7Bk%5Cin%20S%7Dp(i,k)p(k,j).%0A"></p>
</section>
<section id="connection-with-matrix-multiplication" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="connection-with-matrix-multiplication">Connection with matrix multiplication</h2>
<p>By the definition of matrix multiplication,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A(P%5E2)_%7Bij%7D%0A=%0A%5Csum_%7Bk%5Cin%20S%7DP_%7Bik%7DP_%7Bkj%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>Since (P_{ik}=p(i,k)) and (P_{kj}=p(k,j)), we obtain</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cboxed%7Bp%5E%7B(2)%7D(i,j)=(P%5E2)_%7Bij%7D%7D.%0A"></p>
</section>
<section id="intuition" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>Each term</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0Ap(i,k)p(k,j)%0A"></p>
<p>is the probability of following the particular path</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0Ai%20%5Clongrightarrow%20k%20%5Clongrightarrow%20j.%0A"></p>
<p>Because the intermediate state (k) can be any state, we add the probabilities of all possible two-step paths.</p>


</section>

 ]]></description>
